course: dictionary
lesson: 11122
title: Whole: all of it, with nothing missing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Whole means all of something, with no part missing. «A shop sells only whole loaves» means you cannot buy half a loaf, only the full loaf. Whole can also be a noun: a whole is a complete thing made of parts.
explain: «Losing or changing one small word can change a whole meaning.» Here the whole meaning is the complete meaning, with every part working together. One small change can spoil it. To check a word, ask yourself: is any part missing?
explain: Whole also appears in phrases. «The whole class» means every child in the class. «On the whole» means mostly, but not always: «On the whole, the bus is on time» means it is usually on time. Context tells you which meaning you need.
fact: Whole means all of it, with no part missing.
fact: A whole is a complete thing made of parts, so two halves make a whole.
fact: A whole number is a number like 0, 1, 2 or 3, with no fraction or decimal part.
fact: «A fraction like one third means: split the whole set into equal parts and take one part.»
fact: «Whole» sounds like «hole», but the spelling and meaning are different.
exercise: «A shop sells only whole loaves.» What does whole mean here? | *Complete, with no part missing | Very large | Sliced into pieces | Half price
exercise: What is a whole number? | Number with a fraction or decimal part | A number that is always negative | *A number like 0, 1, 2 or 3 with no fraction or decimal part | A number that is written in words
exercise: «On the whole, the bus is on time.» What does on the whole mean? | The bus is late | Only the driver knows | Every single bus is on time | *Mostly, though not always
test: What does «the whole class» mean? | *Every child in the class | The teacher alone | Only the tallest child | Half of the class
test: «Two halves make a whole.» What is a whole in this sentence? | An empty space | *A complete thing made of parts | A single half | A broken piece
exam: Which word completes this? The shop sells only ___ loaves, never half ones. | half | cheap | sliced | *whole
